<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kodiak Island</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kodiak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kodiak Island is a large island off the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>, home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alutiiq">Alutiiq</a> people. In 1784 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-shelikhov">Grigory Shelikhov</a> founded the first permanent Russian settlement in America at Three Saints Bay there, and the town of Kodiak was the capital of Russian America until 1808, when the headquarters moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitka">New Archangel</a> (Sitka).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kodiak-island.jpg" alt="Kodiak Island" width="320" height="273" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karl Musser · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kodiak_Island_map_in_Alaska.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kodiak Island is a large island on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>, separated from the mainland by the Shelikof Strait. It is the largest island of the Kodiak Archipelago, about 160 kilometres long and between 16 and 97 kilometres wide, with an area of 9,311 square kilometres, slightly larger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. It is mountainous and heavily forested in the north and east and fairly treeless in the south, with many deep, ice-free bays that give boats sheltered anchorage. The Kodiak bear and the king crab are native to the island, and the Karluk River is known for its salmon run.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kodiak is the ancestral land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alutiiq">Sugpiaq</a>, an Alutiiq nation, whose people lived by hunting marine mammals, fishing and gathering. The Russian fur trader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-shelikhov">Grigory Shelikhov</a> founded a settlement at Three Saints Bay in 1784, and in 1792 it was moved to the site of the present town of Kodiak, which became the centre of Russian fur trading with Alaska Natives. In 1784 Shelikhov and 130 Russian fur traders massacred several hundred Qik'rtarmiut Sugpiat men, women and children at Refuge Rock, a small island off Sitkalidak Island, which the Alutiiq now call Awa'uq, to become numb.</p><p>Russian traders conscripted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alutiiq">Alutiiq</a> to hunt, gather and process food and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, taking labour by hostage taking, physical threat and punishment. Men were forced to meet quotas of otter pelts and bird skins, and families went hungry and were separated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> mission was authorised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> in 1793 and established on the island in 1794 by monks from the Valaam Monastery. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic from 1837 to 1839 destroyed an estimated one-third of the Native population. After the 1867 Alaska Purchase the island became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. On 6 to 8 June 1912 the eruption of Novarupta, about 160 kilometres to the northwest, covered the island in ash, collapsed roofs and devastated wildlife.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city of Kodiak is the main settlement among seven communities on the island, and ferry and air services connect it with the outside world. Fishing for Pacific salmon, halibut and crab is the most important economic activity. Most of the southwestern two-thirds of the island lies in the Kodiak National Wildlife Refuge, and the island hosts a large United States Coast Guard base.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kodiak Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Vitus Bering's voyage of 1741, Siberian traders hunted sea otters in the Aleutian Islands, and Grigory Shelikhov founded a permanent settlement on <b>Kodiak Island</b> in 1784.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
